Q: Is there any solution for solving pattern in two loops for my pattern? A: <p>The assignment is interesting. It is good that you tried to do it yourself. In my opinion your question should not be down-voted.</p>
<p>However for starters always try to write more generic functions.</p>
<p>For example if the last outputed letter in the pattern will be set to <strong>'I'</strong>  then you will need to rewrite entirely your code because such statements with the magic number <strong>13</strong> like for example</p>
<pre><code>for(i=1;i&lt;=13;i++){
</code></pre>
<p>or</p>
<pre><code>int nRows = 13;
</code></pre>
<p>do not make  sense.</p>
<p>It is evident that the pattern is defined by the last letter that will be outputed. In your code the last outputed letter is <strong>'G'</strong> however as mentioned above the user could want to display the pattern when the last letter is for example <strong>'I'</strong> or even <strong>'Z'</strong>. It is inefficient and error prone to modify the code each time when the user will have a desire to output the pattern using its own specified letter.</p>
<p>So it will ne much better to write a separate function that outputs the pattern for any given last letter.</p>
<p>Thus let's write such a function.</p>
<p>Pay attention to that the function will be more universal if it would allow to output the pattern not only on console but also in a file.</p>
<p>So the function declaration can look the following way</p>
<pre><code>FILE * build_pattern( char last_char, FILE *fp );
</code></pre>
<p>The function can be called for example like</p>
<pre><code>build_pattern( 'G', stdout );
</code></pre>
<p>or even like</p>
<pre><code>build_pattern( 'g', stdout );
</code></pre>
<p>In the last case the function itself will convert the lower case letter <strong>'g'</strong> to upper case letter <strong>'G'</strong>.</p>
<p>To output leading spaces in intermediate lines you can use only a single call of <strong>printf</strong> using the length modifier. For example</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;ctype.h&gt;


FILE * build_pattern( char last_char, FILE *fp )
{
    if ( isalpha( ( unsigned char )last_char ) )
    {
        last_char = toupper( ( unsigned char )last_char );

        char first_char = 'A';

        // Calculate the maximum number of elements that can be
        // outpute in the first and last lines of the pattern
        int n = last_char - first_char + 1;

        for ( int i = 0, spaces = 1; i &lt; 2 * n - 1; i++ )
        {
            fprintf( fp, &quot;%*c&quot;, spaces, first_char );

            fputc( '\n', fp );

            int step = i &lt; n  - 1 ? 1 : - 1;

            spaces += step;
            first_char += step;
        }
    }

    return fp;
}

int main(void) 
{
    while ( 1 )
    {
        char c;
        printf( &quot;Enter a letter: &quot; );

        if ( scanf( &quot; %c&quot;, &amp;c ) != 1 || !isalpha( ( unsigned char )c ) ) break;

        putchar( '\n' );

        fputc( '\n', build_pattern( c, stdout ) );
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The program output might look  the following way</p>
<pre><code>Enter a letter: g

A
 B
  C
   D
    E
     F
      G
     F
    E
   D
  C
 B
A

Enter a letter: *
</code></pre>
<p>Now what we need to do is to write an inner for loop that will output  each row filled with all required elements after the first outputed letter according to the pattern.</p>
<p>Pay attention to that outputed numbers can be greater than <strong>9</strong> when the specified last letter is greater than <strong>'J'</strong>.</p>
<p>In this case gaps between elements will be equal to <strong>2</strong> instead of <strong>1</strong>.</p>
<p>Taking all that into account the function can look  the following way.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;ctype.h&gt;

FILE * build_pattern( char last_char, FILE *fp )
{
    if ( isalpha( ( unsigned char )last_char ) )
    {
        last_char = toupper( ( unsigned char )last_char );

        char first_char = 'A';

        // Calculate the maximum number of elements that can be
        // outpute in the first and last lines of the pattern
        int n = last_char - first_char + 1;

        int length_mod = n &lt; 10 ? 2 : 3;

        for ( int i = 0, spaces = 1; i &lt; 2 * n - 1; i++ )
        {
            fprintf( fp, &quot;%*c&quot;, spaces, first_char );

            for ( int j = 0; j &lt; n - ( first_char - 'A' + 1 ); j++ )
            {
                const char *fmt = j % 2 == 0 ? &quot;%*d&quot; : &quot;%*c&quot;;
                int value = j % 2 == 0 
                    ? first_char - 'A' + 2 + j 
                    : first_char + j + 1;

                fprintf( fp, fmt, length_mod, value );
            }

            fputc( '\n', fp );

            int step = i &lt; n  - 1 ? 1 : - 1;

            spaces += length_mod * step;
            first_char += step;
        }
    }

    return fp;
}

int main(void) 
{
    while ( 1 )
    {
        char c;
        printf( &quot;Enter a letter: &quot; );

        if ( scanf( &quot; %c&quot;, &amp;c ) != 1 || !isalpha( ( unsigned char )c ) ) break;

        putchar( '\n' );

        fputc( '\n', build_pattern( c, stdout ) );
    }
}
</code></pre>
