Q: How do browsers choose between multiple favicon images? A: <p>Answer: If you want to use pixel art as a favicon, only use one.</p>
<p>I tried to mess around with favicons at <a href="https://ivaalo.neocities.org/other/faviconTest/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this address</a>. I put all different sizes I was suggested and found out only the biggest one is chosen. However, there's a limit: if I remove the 96×96 version and above, the tab favicon is always the 16×16 version.</p>
<p>This may be a glitch from Firefox, but I noticed that, when enabling all favicons, there's a fraction of second where the 16×16 version appears, before going back to the 512×512 version.</p>
<p>Chrome use the 32×32 version.</p>
<p>I found out that pixel art favicons are 64×64 icons that are able to be resized to 16×16 (so basically, pixelated scaled from 16 to 64). <a href="https://libresprite.github.io/#!/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">LibreSprite's website</a> is a good example.</p>
<p>To sum up</p>
<ul>
<li><p>If you want a pixel art: do a 16×16 favicon that you'll rescale (with no interpolation) to 64×64, and any other dimensions. My mistake was to use two different pixel arts and have the 32×32 as the base of higher dimensions.</p>
</li>
<li><p>If you don't want to use a pixel art: don't worry and do any scale you want, as long as it doesn't appear blurry.</p>
</li>
<li><p>For both, it's better to have any recommended sizes, as it shouldn't appear blurry:</p>
</li>
</ul>
